WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.620
On today's AI briefing, we think the near-term risk from NVIDIA's leadership change is policy, not product.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:18.160
Unverified China claims and a reported Senate request could reshape export rules faster than any roadmap.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.520
This is the AI briefing for Friday, November 13. I'm Michael.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.840
Like everything here, this briefing is written and read by AI from public sources.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.900
Today, a leadership change at NVIDIA, a cheaper model from Google, a data breach, and a very large infrastructure bet.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:46.100
Jensen Huang has resigned as NVIDIA's C EO Coletcress has been named interim chief.

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:53.980
Google has launched Gemini 4 Hydrogen. It nearly matches Sonnet 5.5 at a lower cost.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:01:00.100
A breach at Fireworks AI exposed chat transcripts from Notion customers.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.440
It shows how much customer data can pass through a third-party inference provider.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:15.380
We'll also look at Nebius, which plans a $40 billion AI infrastructure expansion paid for with prepayments and loans.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.220
First the main stories, then a few quick hits.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.300
First, Stephen's take on the day.

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:28.720
The rush for compute is going to take some into uncharted territory filled with pirates.

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:36.160
First story, and it's an update on one we've been following for four days now.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.180
Jensen Huang has resigned as NVIDIA's CEO, and Colette Kress is interim chief.

00:01:42.660 --> 00:01:44.220
The resignation is confirmed.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:46.660
Much of what surrounds it isn't.

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:51.560
Reports tying NVIDIA to China lobbying and smuggling remain unverified,

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:55.400
and the revenue figures circulating with them lack evidence.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:59.400
There's also a reported request for Senate banking testimony.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:05.420
The calm reading is that a new CEO changes the top job, not the products,

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:11.760
and with an 85% share per exponential view, customers have few alternatives anyway.

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:15.980
We still think the near-term risk is policy, not product.

00:02:15.980 --> 00:02:24.760
Until the probes report, single-vendor compute plans look weaker, and startups offering second-source inference have a clearer pitch.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.980
Here's Stephen's take, One Person Matters.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.140
But how much, now we will find out.

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:39.280
At Alphabet, the lesson was maybe it cements the status quo more than one would like longer term.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:43.400
Second story.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.440
The price of a near-frontier model just fell again.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.380
Google has launched Gemini 4 Hydrogen.

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:57.800
On Artificial Analysis Intelligence Index, it nearly ties Claude Sonnet 5.5 and GPT-6.1.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:05.140
It costs roughly a third of Sonnet's price, and it's 2.5 times cheaper than GPT-6 saw.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:14.640
Simon Willison lists input pricing at $1.50M. He notes a separate rate of $0.375 for the carbon

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.780
and argon tiers from the start of December. Google's Robbie Stein went further on search,

00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:26.260
calling it the biggest search model upgrade. So how solid is this? The performance numbers come

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:31.880
from a third-party index, which helps. But Google's quality claims rest on its own demo,

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:35.720
and the competitor price comparisons come without much context.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.660
Treat the quality story as reported, not proven.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.160
Here's how we see it.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:43.760
This extends the week's pricing slide.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.840
Near Frontier Index scores for roughly a third of Sonnet 5.5's price.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:51.620
That helps startups that charge per task

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:55.060
because their margins widen as model inputs get cheaper.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.720
Companies reselling tokens lose.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:04.500
An agent or vertical app startup paying Sonic class prices could cut model cost by about two-thirds.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:11.240
That holds only if its own evals hold up on the new model, and that's the number we'd want to see.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:14.080
The AI Realist reading is more cautious.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.520
An index near tie is not parity in production.

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.160
The tiered pricing is confusing enough to change the cost of any particular workload.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.840
On that reading, the right move is to wait for hands-on evals.

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:31.780
Here's Stephen's take, Launch.

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.340
Release, repeat until others get tired of it, i.e. run out of money.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.360
That falling price raises a question about where the inference actually runs,

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:46.580
and our third story makes it pointed.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:52.240
Yesterday, TechCrunch reported that Fireworks AI was hit by a cyberattack.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:56.240
Hackers access chat transcripts belonging to Notion's customers.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:05:01.520
Fireworks runs a hosted platform for serving and fine-tuning open-source models.

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:09.400
It competes with Together AI, and it gives businesses like Notion an alternative to closed IP is from OpenAI.

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:13.180
This is its second data breach within two months.

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:14.880
The core facts are solid.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.020
Both Fireworks and Notion have disclosed the breach.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:23.760
What we only have from the companies is how far it reached beyond Notion and what the attackers wanted.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:28.500
So the breach is real, and its full scope is not yet independently known.

00:05:29.180 --> 00:05:38.340
Our view is that inference subprocessors, the outside vendors that process a customer's data on a company's behalf, are now a liability line.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:42.800
A second breach in two months doesn't land only on fireworks.

00:05:43.460 --> 00:05:46.440
It lands on Notion's relationship with its own customers.

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:52.440
That opens a door for providers who can prove tenant isolation and for self-hosted options.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:57.440
For early-stage companies, the effect may show up in enterprise security reviews.

00:05:57.440 --> 00:06:01.440
A startup routing customer data through third-party inference

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:04.440
may be asked about its subprocessor's breach history,

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:08.440
their notification terms, and which provider it would move to.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:14.440
A question worth asking might be whether a founder already has that exit provider tested

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:22.940
tested or only named from who processes the data to who pays for the hardware

00:06:22.940 --> 00:06:29.140
the fourth and last of our main stories is nebius yesterday tech crunch reported

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:36.640
its plan for 40 to 45 billion dollars of capital spending in 2027 up to 85% of

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:41.260
that would be covered by customer prepayments and loans those loans are

00:06:41.260 --> 00:06:48.300
secured against contracts with Microsoft and Meta. Meanwhile, a third-party Microsoft site in New

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:54.880
Jersey has slipped to the second quarter of 2027. The reporting holds up well. Two caveats, though.

00:06:55.340 --> 00:07:01.660
The 85% coverage is Nebius's own projection, and Microsoft hasn't confirmed new dates for the

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:07.060
delayed site. We'll be candid that this cuts partly against our view that AI infrastructure

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:13.280
is leaning too hard on debt. Here, cash-rich counterparties stand behind much of the plan.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:19.540
The New Jersey slip shows where it strains, building the capacity, not paying for it.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:25.940
There's a harder implication for smaller players. GPU cloud startups without hyperscaler anchor

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:31.620
contracts can't copy this kind of prepayment-backed financing. That may push them toward niche

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:38.580
workloads or toward a sale. The market reading is more bullish than ours. Microsoft and Meta

00:07:38.580 --> 00:07:46.300
prepaying for capacity is demand with cash behind it. And a neocloud projecting up to 85%

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:51.920
customer-backed capex is, on that view, a strong signal of revenue quality.

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:56.980
Two quick ones before the close.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:07:59.540
First, back to NVIDIA.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:07.000
Before Huang's exit, asset managers pressed NVIDIA to raise its 25% residual value backstop

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:11.420
on a planned $500 billion AI financing vehicle.

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:15.580
That's a promise to cover part of the hardware's value if it falls.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:21.560
It matters because the financing questions don't leave with the CEO second.

00:08:21.920 --> 00:08:29.480
Myerndal, founded by former Anthropic researchers to build self-improving AI, raised a $1 billion

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:34.080
series A. Klainer Perkins and Clear Lake Capital led it.

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:38.860
The report doesn't say what the company has shown so far, which matters for around

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:41.820
that size.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:45.020
To close, a question worth asking this week.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:49.720
Which third-party inference providers touch customer data, and what's the fallback if

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:55.160
one is breached or cut off. The open question to watch is whether the board probe or the

00:08:55.160 --> 00:09:03.160
senate inquiry confirms China smuggling ties at NVIDIA. Findings, testimony, or export rule action

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:10.680
would settle it. That's the briefing. Stay with me. Right after this, Bella takes you through

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:17.560
today's deep dive on when the platform buys the AI Point product. You can also read it or listen to

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:23.160
it on its own on the AI Briefing webpage where you'll find the written edition in every past

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:37.320
episode. The link is in the show notes. Thanks Michael. This is the AI Briefing Deep Dive for

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:44.440
Friday, November 13, when the platform buys the AI Point product. I'm Bella. Like the briefing,

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.700
It is written and read by AI from public sources.

00:09:50.620 --> 00:09:54.020
Ada, the Toronto customer service AI company,

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:58.420
is merging with Dialpad, a California communications platform.

00:09:59.040 --> 00:10:02.580
The deal is expected to close on or around November 12.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:05.460
The Globe and Mail first reported it,

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:10.400
and says the all-share deal values Ada at approximately $600 million CAD.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:13.260
Neither company has disclosed terms.

00:10:14.440 --> 00:10:17.520
In US dollars, that is roughly $430 million.

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:22.660
Against the $1.2 billion ADA was worth after its 2021 series,

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:24.800
see the control signal is just as clear.

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:29.480
Dialpad CEO, Craig Walker, runs the combined company,

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:33.460
and ADA's co-founder, Mike Murchison, becomes chief AI officer.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:35.580
ADA is not alone this year.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:37.480
Zendesk bought Forethought.

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:40.020
So this is a good week to ask two questions.

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:45.940
why do ai customer service point products keep ending up inside platforms

00:10:45.940 --> 00:10:50.620
and when the price comes in below the last round who actually gets paid

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:58.340
start with the product ada began in 2016 building customer service chatbots for other companies

00:10:58.340 --> 00:11:04.040
when large language models arrived it rebuilt around ai agents and cut staff along the way

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:08.300
Dialpad sells phones, contact center software, and sales tools.

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:14.360
In October 2025, Dialpad launched a platform for building autonomous voice and text agents.

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:16.700
That rate card is the core of the problem.

00:11:17.260 --> 00:11:19.160
A point product sells one capability.

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:21.480
Here the agent that answers the customer.

00:11:22.380 --> 00:11:28.360
A platform owns the phone line, the human agents, the call recordings, and the billing relationship.

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:31.780
Once the platform ships an agent priced near a dollar,

00:11:32.240 --> 00:11:35.140
the point product has to justify a premium on its own.

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:36.860
Now the exit math.

00:11:37.380 --> 00:11:40.340
A liquidation preference is the investor's right to be repaid

00:11:40.340 --> 00:11:44.860
before common shareholders, mostly founders and employees, see anything.

00:11:45.620 --> 00:11:48.400
The standard term is one times non-participating.

00:11:49.240 --> 00:11:51.920
The investor either takes back what they put in

00:11:51.920 --> 00:11:56.340
or converts to common and takes their ownership share, whichever pays more.

00:11:57.020 --> 00:11:58.000
Here is a worked example.

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:04.700
and it is hypothetical because ADA's terms are not public. Say the 2021 investors put in $130

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:10.980
million at a $1.2 billion valuation. That buys them roughly a tenth of the company.

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:15.360
So they take the preference and get $130 million back instead.

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:21.740
That money comes off the top, ahead of everyone holding common. If most of it sits in preferences

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:26.620
that rank ahead of common, common holders split what is left, after the preferred holders have

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:32.600
recovered their money. One more wrinkle matters. This is an all-share deal. Nobody receives cash.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:37.800
The Globe and Mail reports the deal as an all-share transaction, so Ada's holders are

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:43.040
paid in shares rather than cash, and neither company has disclosed the terms. The headline

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:50.220
number is only as real as that price. Here is what is established. Both companies announce

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:56.780
the combination themselves in a dial pad press release and in ada's own posts dial pad's release

00:12:56.780 --> 00:13:01.820
says the combined company serves over 50 000 companies and that customers keep their current

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:08.380
contracts and pricing those figures are self-reported the 2021 round is well documented

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:13.980
some reports name tiger global as lead but the logic and contemporary coverage names spark

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:20.880
dial pads per conversation prices come straight from its own rate card now what is reported but

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:26.780
not confirmed the 600 million dollars cad valuation comes from the globe and mail and

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:32.600
the companies have not confirmed it the globe and mail reported that ada's recent annual revenue is

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:39.820
near us dollar 50 million which puts the 430 million dollar deal at about 8.6 times revenue

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:46.680
It also reports that Ada cut half its marketing team in July and another 25 positions before the

00:13:46.680 --> 00:13:52.480
announcement. Treat those as press reports, not filings. And here is what nobody has shown.

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:57.880
We don't know Ada's preference stack, its seniority order, or whether any round carried

00:13:57.880 --> 00:14:02.800
terms above one times. We don't know the price per dial pad share used in the exchange,

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:09.700
so the $430 million is a paper figure. We don't know what Ada's revenue was growing at,

00:14:09.820 --> 00:14:14.280
or how much of it was LLM agent revenue rather than legacy chatbot contracts.

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:16.600
One detail deserves attention.

00:14:17.320 --> 00:14:20.320
Dialpad's release says both companies are backed by investors,

00:14:20.500 --> 00:14:22.580
including Andresen Horowitz and Bessemer.

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.080
Bessemer was in Ada Series C,

00:14:25.440 --> 00:14:28.820
when the same investor sits on both sides of a stock-for-stock merger.

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:31.700
Its interest in the exchange ratio is mixed.

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:35.040
That is a reason to read the terms carefully once they are known.

00:14:35.980 --> 00:14:38.180
It is not evidence of anything wrong.

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:41.820
Finally, the market for this category is split in two.

00:14:43.580 --> 00:14:46.440
The same facts support several honest readings.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:52.000
The AGI forward reading says agents are getting good enough to take over real labor budgets in support.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:57.020
In that world, the winners are priced on the work they replace, not on software seats.

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:01.220
On this reading, ADA did not fail because the category is weak.

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:05.980
It fell behind faster, better-funded rivals in a market that is still forming.

00:15:06.620 --> 00:15:09.100
The A realist reading puts more weight on the plumbing.

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:14.440
The agent itself is converging on a commodity built on the same frontier models.

00:15:15.040 --> 00:15:18.120
The hard parts are the phone network, the handoff to a human,

00:15:18.560 --> 00:15:20.660
the customer record, and the integration work.

00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:25.580
BetaKit also notes surveys suggesting many consumers would still rather speak to a human.

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:29.040
If so, the agent is a feature of the contact center,

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:32.380
and the platform that owns the contact center captures it.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.300
The market reading is plainer.

00:15:34.840 --> 00:15:37.000
Ada was priced at the 2021 peak.

00:15:38.220 --> 00:15:41.700
What changed is the multiple, not necessarily the business.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:43.740
The policy angle is narrower.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:48.820
A Canadian AI company with a Toronto base is now run from California.

00:15:49.580 --> 00:15:54.260
Reports say Dialped plans to keep Ada's Canadian presence and most of its staff.

00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:57.040
How long that lasts is an open question.

00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:02.980
Our read is that this deal is about distribution and control more than technology.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:05.640
Dialpad already had an agent and a price.

00:16:06.160 --> 00:16:07.860
What it bought was Ada's deployed agents,

00:16:08.300 --> 00:16:11.860
its enterprise accounts, and its team, paid for in its own stock.

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:16.260
For a seed or Series A company building an AI app on frontier models,

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:18.520
the lesson is about where the wedge sits.

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:23.360
If your customer's platform vendor can ship a near equivalent at a dollar a conversation,

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:26.760
your moat has to be something that vendor cannot easily rebuild.

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:32.800
That could be proprietary workflow data, deep integration into systems of record,

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:36.420
or an outcome guarantee the platform will not underwrite.

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:40.540
For investors, the lesson is that headline exit value

00:16:40.540 --> 00:16:43.120
and common holder value can diverge sharply.

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:45.160
That matters for retention,

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:48.920
because employees' holding options can read a cap table too.

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:52.960
One question worth exploring in diligence might be,

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:56.660
if this company sold tomorrow to the platform it integrates with,

00:16:56.980 --> 00:17:00.220
at a third of its last price, what would common holders receive?

00:17:00.940 --> 00:17:02.140
We'd want to see that answer,

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:06.360
plus the share of revenue that would survive the platform launching its own agent.

00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:08.160
We hold this provisionally.

00:17:08.840 --> 00:17:11.480
Disclosed terms showing a clean preference stack

00:17:11.480 --> 00:17:14.880
or strong growth inside Dialpad would soften it.

00:17:16.540 --> 00:17:18.460
Three things would settle this.

00:17:18.920 --> 00:17:20.100
First, the terms.

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:23.420
If Dialpad raises money or files for an IPO,

00:17:23.820 --> 00:17:26.780
the price per share will show what ADA holders really received.

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:28.340
Second, retention.

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:31.500
Watch whether ADA's senior engineers and Murchison

00:17:31.500 --> 00:17:33.880
are still there a year after the November close,

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:37.600
and whether Dialpad keeps its promise on third-party integrations.

00:17:38.180 --> 00:17:40.520
Those promises currently have no end date.

00:17:41.120 --> 00:17:42.980
Third, the premium end of the market.

00:17:43.940 --> 00:17:47.080
If Sierra and Decagon grow into their valuations,

00:17:47.580 --> 00:17:49.420
the agent layer can stand alone.

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:53.340
If they end up sold to platforms as Finn and Cresta were,

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:55.180
the platform reading wins.

00:17:55.880 --> 00:18:00.080
Their next rounds, or their absence, during 2027 will tell us.

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:04.860
That's the deep dive and today's AI briefing.

00:18:05.600 --> 00:18:07.600
Every source is linked in the show notes,

00:18:07.600 --> 00:18:10.820
and the AI briefing webpage has the written edition,

00:18:11.280 --> 00:18:13.640
this deep dive and every past episode.

